1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,469
What happened to the typing pool?

2
00:00:03,073 --> 00:00:06,173
In 1970, about three point nine million

3
00:00:06,231 --> 00:00:09,424
Americans were stenographers, typists, or

4
00:00:09,516 --> 00:00:10,561
secretaries.

5
00:00:11,165 --> 00:00:13,882
Today, typing for a living is projected to

6
00:00:13,963 --> 00:00:15,623
shrink thirty-four percent.

7
00:00:16,227 --> 00:00:17,969
Who gets the next door?

8
00:00:21,983 --> 00:00:24,433
At dusk, the lamplighter passes an office

9
00:00:24,468 --> 00:00:24,932
block.

10
00:00:25,373 --> 00:00:27,904
On the third floor, rows of green desk

11
00:00:27,997 --> 00:00:30,435
lamps, and a sound comes down to the

12
00:00:30,482 --> 00:00:34,359
street: keys striking, a small bell, the

13
00:00:34,417 --> 00:00:36,519
slap of a carriage return.

14
00:00:37,215 --> 00:00:39,166
This was the typing pool.

15
00:00:43,383 --> 00:00:45,380
The typewriter made a new kind of job

16
00:00:45,496 --> 00:00:46,796
almost overnight.

17
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:50,837
Letters, contracts, invoices, all of it

18
00:00:50,918 --> 00:00:53,577
now had to be typed, and someone had to

19
00:00:53,635 --> 00:00:55,910
take it down first in shorthand.

20
00:00:56,607 --> 00:00:58,441
In 1900, the U.S.

21
00:00:58,488 --> 00:01:00,542
census counted about one hundred

22
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:02,887
thirty-four thousand stenographers,

23
00:01:02,992 --> 00:01:05,000
typists, and secretaries.

24
00:01:05,407 --> 00:01:08,402
By 1930, about one point one million.

25
00:01:08,959 --> 00:01:12,048
By 1950, about one point seven million.

26
00:01:12,605 --> 00:01:15,124
The census itself warns that the 1900

27
00:01:15,194 --> 00:01:17,702
figures are approximations, and that

28
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:20,616
definitions shifted from count to count.

29
00:01:21,161 --> 00:01:23,913
So read the early line as a shape, not a

30
00:01:23,971 --> 00:01:25,004
precise measure.

31
00:01:25,515 --> 00:01:27,593
And the shape is a boom.

32
00:01:29,383 --> 00:01:31,554
Most of those new jobs went to women.

33
00:01:31,995 --> 00:01:34,375
Across all clerical work, the census

34
00:01:34,433 --> 00:01:36,407
counted about two hundred twelve thousand

35
00:01:36,465 --> 00:01:37,940
women in 1900.

36
00:01:38,450 --> 00:01:42,224
By 1970, about ten and a half million.

37
00:01:42,827 --> 00:01:45,788
By then, women held nearly three in four

38
00:01:45,881 --> 00:01:47,622
of all clerical jobs.

39
00:01:48,133 --> 00:01:50,420
For millions of women, the office was the

40
00:01:50,490 --> 00:01:52,649
first door into paid work outside the

41
00:01:52,719 --> 00:01:55,296
home, and the typewriter was the key that

42
00:01:55,343 --> 00:01:56,179
opened it.

43
00:01:57,967 --> 00:01:59,616
And the pool kept filling.

44
00:02:00,301 --> 00:02:03,586
After the war, offices grew, paperwork

45
00:02:03,644 --> 00:02:06,338
grew, and so did the people who typed it.

46
00:02:07,139 --> 00:02:09,449
The census changed its job categories

47
00:02:09,507 --> 00:02:12,839
again in 1970, so the next decade is drawn

48
00:02:12,874 --> 00:02:13,989
as its own line.

49
00:02:14,488 --> 00:02:18,493
On the 1970 basis, stenographers, typists,

50
00:02:18,528 --> 00:02:21,489
and secretaries numbered about two point

51
00:02:21,535 --> 00:02:25,030
three million in 1960, and about three

52
00:02:25,099 --> 00:02:27,921
point nine million in 1970.

53
00:02:28,524 --> 00:02:31,102
That is up sixty-nine percent in ten

54
00:02:31,171 --> 00:02:31,810
years.

55
00:02:32,506 --> 00:02:34,933
At its peak, the typing pool was one of

56
00:02:34,979 --> 00:02:37,487
the largest workforces in the country.

57
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:41,309
The computer came for the office in two

58
00:02:41,401 --> 00:02:42,098
waves.

59
00:02:42,481 --> 00:02:45,883
In 1960, the BLS studied twenty offices

60
00:02:46,092 --> 00:02:47,717
that had just installed one.

61
00:02:48,321 --> 00:02:50,213
Of about two thousand eight hundred

62
00:02:50,248 --> 00:02:53,035
employees affected, only nine had been

63
00:02:53,081 --> 00:02:54,451
laid off a year later.

64
00:02:55,055 --> 00:02:57,388
Most were moved, or stayed put.

65
00:02:57,899 --> 00:03:00,001
The first machines went for the routine

66
00:03:00,059 --> 00:03:03,356
records: posting, checking, filing,

67
00:03:03,472 --> 00:03:04,180
computing.

68
00:03:04,691 --> 00:03:06,955
Only a small share of the affected workers

69
00:03:07,001 --> 00:03:09,637
did correspondence, stenographic, or

70
00:03:09,788 --> 00:03:10,972
secretarial work.

71
00:03:11,576 --> 00:03:14,002
The typing pool was spared, for a while.

72
00:03:14,443 --> 00:03:16,893
The second wave sat on every desk.

73
00:03:17,404 --> 00:03:21,200
By 1988, typists, word processors, and

74
00:03:21,270 --> 00:03:23,882
data entry keyers held about one point

75
00:03:23,964 --> 00:03:27,563
four million jobs, and the BLS wrote: with

76
00:03:27,609 --> 00:03:29,606
the proliferation of personal computers

77
00:03:29,664 --> 00:03:32,126
throughout the economy, more and more

78
00:03:32,172 --> 00:03:34,471
workers are performing work formerly done

79
00:03:34,517 --> 00:03:35,678
by typists.

80
00:03:39,167 --> 00:03:40,897
The lamplighter walks on.

81
00:03:41,593 --> 00:03:43,648
The third-floor windows are dark now,

82
00:03:43,823 --> 00:03:46,203
except for one screen, its cursor

83
00:03:46,272 --> 00:03:46,911
blinking.

84
00:03:47,607 --> 00:03:50,138
No bells, no carriage returns.

85
00:03:50,835 --> 00:03:53,018
Somewhere in the building, words still

86
00:03:53,053 --> 00:03:54,353
appear on a page.

87
00:03:55,049 --> 00:03:57,186
Fewer hands are typing them.

88
00:04:03,467 --> 00:04:06,172
Today, the job title that grew out of the

89
00:04:06,219 --> 00:04:09,644
typing pool, word processors and typists,

90
00:04:09,887 --> 00:04:11,582
is one of the fastest-declining

91
00:04:11,640 --> 00:04:13,904
occupations on the BLS list.

92
00:04:14,229 --> 00:04:16,354
It is projected to shrink from about forty

93
00:04:16,424 --> 00:04:19,837
thousand jobs in 2025 to about twenty-six

94
00:04:19,895 --> 00:04:21,671
thousand in 2035.

95
00:04:21,950 --> 00:04:24,353
That is down thirty-four percent.

96
00:04:24,864 --> 00:04:27,616
Data entry keyers, the people who type

97
00:04:27,651 --> 00:04:30,147
information into systems, are projected to

98
00:04:30,216 --> 00:04:32,631
fall twenty-five point five percent, from

99
00:04:32,678 --> 00:04:35,023
about one hundred thirty-two thousand to

100
00:04:35,104 --> 00:04:36,660
about ninety-eight thousand.

101
00:04:37,101 --> 00:04:39,829
Both are projections, not counts.

102
00:04:41,633 --> 00:04:43,804
The largest descendant of the typing pool

103
00:04:43,874 --> 00:04:44,733
is much bigger.

104
00:04:45,174 --> 00:04:48,448
In 2025, there were about three point five

105
00:04:48,518 --> 00:04:50,944
million secretaries and administrative

106
00:04:50,967 --> 00:04:51,792
assistants.

107
00:04:52,233 --> 00:04:54,613
The BLS projects a decline of about two

108
00:04:54,683 --> 00:04:58,514
percent by 2035, to about three point four

109
00:04:58,583 --> 00:04:59,152
million.

110
00:04:59,849 --> 00:05:02,914
Its explanation is direct: technology,

111
00:05:03,065 --> 00:05:05,480
including artificial intelligence systems

112
00:05:05,538 --> 00:05:08,475
and digital tools, enables staff in many

113
00:05:08,533 --> 00:05:10,333
organizations to prepare their own

114
00:05:10,379 --> 00:05:13,293
documents without the help of secretaries.

115
00:05:13,990 --> 00:05:16,590
The boss who once dictated a letter now

116
00:05:16,672 --> 00:05:19,110
writes it, with a machine helping.

117
00:05:20,925 --> 00:05:23,154
The clearest case is the one closest to

118
00:05:23,224 --> 00:05:25,697
the old stenographer, who turned spoken

119
00:05:25,755 --> 00:05:27,531
words into typed pages.

120
00:05:28,228 --> 00:05:30,457
Medical transcriptionists do that work

121
00:05:30,515 --> 00:05:32,175
today, for doctors.

122
00:05:32,686 --> 00:05:36,076
In its January 2026 review, the BLS

123
00:05:36,169 --> 00:05:38,491
projected their employment to decline four

124
00:05:38,549 --> 00:05:42,636
point nine percent from 2024 to 2034, and

125
00:05:42,717 --> 00:05:46,351
it named the reason: AI technology can

126
00:05:46,409 --> 00:05:49,265
recognize speech and transcribe audio.

127
00:05:49,869 --> 00:05:51,808
That comes from an earlier projection

128
00:05:51,866 --> 00:05:54,223
cycle than the other numbers, and it is a

129
00:05:54,281 --> 00:05:56,684
forecast, not the end of the job.

130
00:05:58,467 --> 00:06:01,207
What does AI do to writing work itself?

131
00:06:01,811 --> 00:06:04,992
In a 2023 experiment, MIT economists

132
00:06:05,108 --> 00:06:08,243
Shakked Noy and Whitney Zhang gave four

133
00:06:08,324 --> 00:06:10,332
hundred forty-four college-educated

134
00:06:10,379 --> 00:06:13,397
professionals short writing tasks: press

135
00:06:13,455 --> 00:06:16,869
releases, short reports, delicate emails.

136
00:06:17,310 --> 00:06:19,957
Half of them, chosen at random, could use

137
00:06:20,004 --> 00:06:21,188
ChatGPT.

138
00:06:21,513 --> 00:06:23,231
Those with the tool finished about

139
00:06:23,289 --> 00:06:25,983
thirty-seven percent faster, ten minutes

140
00:06:26,041 --> 00:06:28,166
off an average of twenty-seven, and

141
00:06:28,235 --> 00:06:30,221
graders rated their work higher.

142
00:06:30,824 --> 00:06:32,926
The weakest writers gained the most.

143
00:06:33,437 --> 00:06:35,213
In the authors' words, the tool

144
00:06:35,295 --> 00:06:37,849
substantially compresses the productivity

145
00:06:37,907 --> 00:06:38,812
distribution.

146
00:06:39,323 --> 00:06:42,005
The study measured tasks, not jobs.

147
00:06:42,446 --> 00:06:44,362
And these were professionals writing for

148
00:06:44,408 --> 00:06:46,672
themselves, not typists.

149
00:06:48,467 --> 00:06:50,719
So here is the comparison, on the same

150
00:06:50,754 --> 00:06:54,992
scale: one source, one period, 2025 to

151
00:06:55,061 --> 00:06:56,188
2035.

152
00:06:56,675 --> 00:06:59,067
Word processors and typists: down

153
00:06:59,148 --> 00:07:00,321
thirty-four percent.

154
00:07:00,750 --> 00:07:03,583
Data entry keyers: down twenty-five point

155
00:07:03,641 --> 00:07:04,524
five percent.

156
00:07:05,011 --> 00:07:07,588
Secretaries and administrative assistants:

157
00:07:07,902 --> 00:07:09,318
down about two percent.

158
00:07:09,829 --> 00:07:12,836
All jobs together: up three point five

159
00:07:12,883 --> 00:07:13,475
percent.

160
00:07:14,078 --> 00:07:16,412
Not the same technology as the typewriter.

161
00:07:16,923 --> 00:07:20,510
The same pattern: a repetitive human task,

162
00:07:20,719 --> 00:07:22,798
taken over by an automated system.

163
00:07:23,494 --> 00:07:26,234
The more a job is pure typing, the steeper

164
00:07:26,408 --> 00:07:27,801
the projected fall.

165
00:07:28,312 --> 00:07:30,704
The more it is organizing, judging and

166
00:07:30,820 --> 00:07:32,875
dealing with people, the smaller.

167
00:07:33,478 --> 00:07:36,068
The typewriter created the pool; the

168
00:07:36,126 --> 00:07:39,237
computer and AI are taking the typing away

169
00:07:39,307 --> 00:07:39,852
from it.

170
00:07:40,363 --> 00:07:42,302
And all four of these numbers are

171
00:07:42,348 --> 00:07:44,938
forecasts, not counts.

172
00:07:46,758 --> 00:07:49,115
The typing pool was more than a room of

173
00:07:49,196 --> 00:07:50,044
machines.

174
00:07:50,554 --> 00:07:52,935
For generations of women, it was a door

175
00:07:53,027 --> 00:07:54,409
into the working world.

176
00:07:54,920 --> 00:07:57,381
The question now is less whether typing

177
00:07:57,474 --> 00:07:58,403
survives.

178
00:07:58,844 --> 00:08:00,678
It is whether the next door is there for

179
00:08:00,736 --> 00:08:03,058
people starting out, and whether the work

180
00:08:03,117 --> 00:08:05,566
that grows around AI is open to them.

181
00:08:05,891 --> 00:08:07,517
Nobody can count that yet.

182
00:08:07,958 --> 00:08:10,350
That is what we will be watching.

183
00:08:14,958 --> 00:08:16,978
Back on the street, the lamplighter

184
00:08:17,094 --> 00:08:18,580
reaches the last post.

185
00:08:19,091 --> 00:08:22,028
The glass opens, the flame takes, and the

186
00:08:22,110 --> 00:08:23,596
corner is lit again.

187
00:08:24,292 --> 00:08:26,916
Every era has its last lamplighters.

188
00:08:27,613 --> 00:08:30,225
Some of them sat in the typing pool, and

189
00:08:30,318 --> 00:08:32,652
millions still keep an office running.

